The '''Economy of Guajicutea''' is a service-led, upper-middle-income economy built around tourism, transport, call centers, port logistics, food processing, and fisheries. In 2024, nominal {{w|GDP}} was estimated at about I$43.2 billion, or roughly I$15,400 per person. The economy is highly urbanized and outward-facing, with its strongest links running to [[Sarta]], [[Lacashe]], [[Asikyira]], and [[Balboa]].

Guajicutea's modern economy emerged out of the post-2000 recovery period. During the Mera era, the state obscured a weakening fiscal position by selling assets such as airports and ports. After 2000, the government had to restructure that hidden debt, negotiate a new [[Adonian Community]] loan, and slowly rebuild credibility. By the 2010s, tourism growth, services expansion, and earlier-than-expected debt repayment gave the island a steadier base. The {{w|recession}} caused by the covid pandemic then interrupted that growth in 2020–2021, but the economy recovered quickly once travel resumed.

== Structure ==

Guajicutea's economy is dominated by {{w|services}}, which account for roughly three-quarters of output. Tourism and hospitality remain the main visible sector, but they are now accompanied by call centers, administrative outsourcing, logistics, retail, and public administration. Industry is smaller but important, especially around port facilities, food processing, light manufacturing, and construction. Agriculture and fisheries remain smaller in GDP terms, yet they still matter for food security, rural employment, and export earnings.

The island's economic geography is concentrated. Waribá is the main port and tourism hub, Tabakurí is the administrative and government center, and the capital corridor links both to the airport and resort districts. Barikú and the western coast support smaller tourism and fisheries activity, while inland valleys and leeward lowlands carry most of the commercial farming.

