== Tourism and services ==

Tourism is the backbone of the modern economy. Guajicutea's beaches, ports, resort districts, and short travel distance from nearby middle-class societies make it a natural holiday destination. The expansion of middle-class travel in [[Lacashe]], [[Asikyira]], and [[Balboa]] has been particularly important since the 2010s. Visitors come for beach holidays, shopping trips, family travel, cruise connections, and short stays centered on Waribá and the capital region.

The tourism sector is closely linked to services. Hotels and restaurants support a large hospitality workforce, while call centers and office services provide a second major urban employment base. Guajicutea became attractive for offshoring because it offered lower labour costs than many neighboring states and a bilingual urban workforce. That made it useful for Sartan companies looking for customer support, reservations, back-office accounting, and administrative work.

The sector is still vulnerable to shocks. The 2019 hurricane damaged port facilities in Waribá and slowed tourism, and the pandemic years showed how quickly travel-dependent income can disappear. Even so, tourism recovered faster than in earlier crises because the sector was now supported by a broader services economy and by stronger regional demand.

== Agriculture and fisheries ==

Agriculture remains smaller than services, but it is still central to everyday life and export variety. Commercial farming is concentrated in inland valleys, coastal plains, and lower-rainfall western districts. Sugar is the main cash crop, followed by cassava products, tobacco, vegetables, and some fruit cultivation. [[Bariña]], a cane spirit distilled from sugar by-products, is one of the best-known examples of that processing chain and remains an important export and heritage product. Agriculture also supports local food supply, which matters in a country that imports a large share of its fuel and machinery.

Fisheries are more visible than the size of the sector alone suggests. Coastal fishing supports domestic consumption and exports seafood, reef fish, dried fish, and related products. The sector is especially important on the east and west coasts, where small harbors and landing points feed local markets and food processing plants. Fisheries also provide seasonal work for households that split income between sea, tourism, and construction.

== Industry and infrastructure ==

Industry in Guajicutea is mostly light and service-linked rather than heavy. Food processing, construction materials, port repair, and small manufacturing matter more than large-scale factory production. The industrial base expanded after the 2000 debt restructuring and then again in the 2010s as tourism demand pulled in construction and transport investment.

Waribá is the center of this activity. Its port, ship-repair facilities, and waterfront logistics make it the country’s main industrial gateway. The 2019 hurricane damaged parts of the port system, but reconstruction helped sustain construction employment and keep investment moving. Industrial employment is also tied to public works, because roads, airport links, and resort infrastructure require constant maintenance in a tropical coastal environment.

== Labour market ==

The labour market is large, urban, and increasingly service-based. Unemployment remains moderate by regional standards, but it is sensitive to tourism downturns, housing costs, and global travel conditions. The pandemic and the harsh lockdown years briefly pushed the economy into recession, but unemployment improved as tourism recovered.

Labour politics returned strongly in the mid-2010s. Strikes in 2016 and 2018 pushed employers and government to accept a stronger wage floor. The resulting {{w|minimum wage}} law tied increases to inflation, which helped prevent pay from falling behind prices in the service economy. Union politics did not return to the confrontational level of the Mera years, but it became a permanent part of economic life again.

Average wages remain above those of the poorer island economies in the region, but housing costs and import dependence reduce how far those wages go in urban areas.

== External trade ==

Guajicutea is a trade-heavy economy. It exports both goods and services, and its imports are essential to its energy, construction, and consumer markets. Tourism is effectively a service export, while call centers and back-office work also generate foreign earnings. On the goods side, sugar, [[Bariña]], seafood, tobacco, cassava flour, textiles, and processed foods form the core export mix.

Imports are centered on fuel, machinery, vehicles, electronics, pharmaceuticals, fertilizers, and construction materials. Like many island economies, Guajicutea depends on external supply chains for high-value manufactured goods and energy. That dependence is one reason why the country keeps reserves relatively high and tries to maintain a stable current account.

