== Public finances ==

The state finances are more stable than they were in the early 2000s. The hidden debt accumulated during the Mera era was brought back onto the books after 2000, and the government spent much of the next decade repairing its balance sheet. The earlier repayment of the Adonian Community loan improved flexibility, but the state still runs with modest deficits and a relatively high dependence on tourism revenue.

Public spending is concentrated in transport, tourism promotion, infrastructure repair, education, health, and old-age support. The last of these has become more important as the population ages and birthrates fall below replacement. Pension politics and the needs of retirees have put new pressure on the budget, especially after housing costs rose in the major cities.

The banking system remains cautious. The central bank maintains a managed floating currency regime, smoothing volatility rather than fixing the exchange rate. Credit is solid but not exceptional, and the country remains exposed to shocks in tourism, fuel prices, and regional travel demand.

== Housing and consumption ==

Housing has become one of the country's most important economic issues. Guajicutea became a popular retirement destination for people from [[Sarta]] in the mid-2010s, and foreign Sartan buyers pushed up home prices in the capital and major coastal cities. By the early 2020s, housing had become a major political problem, especially for younger urban workers and public employees.

That pressure changed consumption patterns. More household income now goes to rent, mortgages, and transport than in earlier decades, while consumer spending remains strong in tourism districts and city centers. The government response has included restrictions on foreign home purchases in some areas and the creation of partial retirement support mechanisms, but the underlying problem remains linked to land scarcity, coastal demand, and the island's popularity as a place to live later in life.

== Currency and banking ==

The Guajicutean sol floats, though the central bank intervenes to limit large swings against the Sartan peso and other regional currencies. The system supports tourism, but it also requires active reserve management because imported fuel and food can quickly change the balance of payments.

The banking sector is small but modern. It serves tourism, trade, construction, and the growing services economy. Credit conditions are fairly stable, with the main risks coming from external shocks rather than domestic banking failures.

== Recent history ==

The economy's recent history can be read as a sequence of shocks and recoveries. The pandemic recession of 2020–2021 hit tourism hard, but the island recovered quickly in 2022. The 2024 coalition shift toward [[Partido Popular Guaji]] and [[Partido Bukaí]] reflected a desire for growth and stability after the stress of housing and pension politics. Guajicutea entered the mid-2020s with a stronger service base than in the past, but also with slower demographic growth and more visible pressure on housing and retirement systems.

